  "why": "The statement is universal over Fano varieties and leaves the exceptional subset unspecified, so a proof of the asymptotic for one variety or class (toric, wonderful compactifications, ax+b compactifications, split quintic del Pezzo surfaces, the chordal cubic fourfold, subvarieties of toric varieties) is a case. Thin-set removals count as cases because the wording ('certain subsets') does not insist on a Zariski-closed removal. For the same reason I scored Gao's counterexamples to the closed-set form (2022, 2023, 2024) as variants. They refute Manin's original stronger form, which the bullet does not commit to, and the record says nothing against the thin-set form. Function-field, geometric-Manin, log/integral, M-point and R-equivalence/Cassels–Swinnerton-Dyer papers answer different questions, so they are variants. Surveys and the M-point conjecture paper are none. Pieropan–Schindler's leading constant is not identified with Peyre's. I still scored it a case, because the bullet never states the constant; its acceptance by Algebra & Number Theory makes it digested. The AI roles are assisted only on the two off-topic variant papers, where the quotes describe models producing lemmas or a key idea. ChatGPT proofreading in Tanimoto 2026 is none.",
  "doubts": "The Gao closed-set counterexamples were the close call between variant and case/refuted. If the bullet is read as Manin's original Zariski-open form, they would be case-level refutations, and the Derenthal 2025 thin-set result would not count. The AI quotes in Kanevsky et al. and Alexeev–Schreieder came from a summarising fetch. Pieropan–Schindler's subvarieties are taken to be Fano by adjunction, as the record reports. The Sra paper (2608.29595) was not read.",
